            <ArticleTitle>Methylation changes and pathways affected in preterm birth: a role for SLC6A3 in neurodevelopment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>91-103</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/epi-2017-0082</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyze whether preterm newborns show differences in methylation patterns in comparison to full-term newborns in white blood cells.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Anthropometrical, biochemical features and methylation levels of preterm newborns (n = 24) and full-term newborns (n = 22) recruited in La Paz University Hospital (Spain) were assessed at 12 months of gestational age, whereas Bayley Scale of Infant Development was evaluated at 24/36 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">From all the statistically significant CpGs, methylation levels of cg00997378 (SLC6A3 gene) showed the highest differences (p &lt; 0.0001), being associated with prematurity risk factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS"> SLC6A3 methylation, previously related to attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, neuronal function and behavior, might be a potential epigenetic biomarker with value in the early diagnosis and management of neurodevelopmental diseases in newborns.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
